### What is the two single numbers problem?

In an array where every number appears twice except two numbers that each appear once, find both single numbers in O(1) space. This extends the basic single-number XOR trick.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class TwoSingleNumbers {

    public static int[] findTwoSingles(int[] nums) {
        // Step 1: XOR all to get combined XOR of the two singles
        int xor = 0;
        for (int n : nums) xor ^= n;

        // Step 2: Isolate rightmost set bit
        int rightmostBit = xor & (-xor);

        // Step 3: Split and XOR each group
        int num1 = 0, num2 = 0;
        for (int n : nums) {
            if ((n & rightmostBit) != 0) {
                num1 ^= n;
            } else {
                num2 ^= n;
            }
        }
        return new int[]{num1, num2};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] entries = {101, 202, 303, 404, 101, 303};
        System.out.println("Two singles: " + Arrays.toString(findTwoSingles(entries)));
    }
}
```

```text
Two singles: [404, 202]
```

### Why does the combined XOR have at least one set bit?

Because the two single numbers are different, their XOR is non-zero. A non-zero XOR means at least one bit position differs between them. That differing bit is the splitter.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CombinedXorDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int a = 202, b = 404;
        int xor = a ^ b;
        System.out.println("202 XOR 404 = " + xor);
        System.out.println("Binary: " + Integer.toBinaryString(xor));
        System.out.println("Non-zero: differing bits exist");
    }
}
```

```text
202 XOR 404 = 342
Binary: 101010110
Non-zero: differing bits exist
```

### How does x & (-x) isolate the rightmost set bit?

Negating in two's complement flips all bits and adds one. AND-ing the original with its negation keeps only the lowest set bit. Any set bit of the combined XOR would work as the splitter; the rightmost is the cheapest to compute.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class RightmostBitDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 342;  // binary: 101010110
        int rightmost = x & (-x);
        System.out.println("x = " + x + " binary: " + Integer.toBinaryString(x));
        System.out.println("Rightmost set bit: " + rightmost
                + " binary: " + Integer.toBinaryString(rightmost));
    }
}
```

```text
x = 342 binary: 101010110
Rightmost set bit: 2 binary: 10
```

### Why does the split guarantee the two singles land in different groups?

The differing bit is set in exactly one of the two singles and not the other (that is what makes it a differing bit). So one single goes into the "bit set" group and the other into the "bit not set" group. Paired values share the same bit value, so each pair lands in the same group and cancels.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SplitGuarantee {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int a = 202, b = 404;
        int xor = a ^ b;
        int bit = xor & (-xor);  // 2

        System.out.println("202 & 2 = " + (202 & bit) + " → group " + ((202 & bit) != 0 ? "A" : "B"));
        System.out.println("404 & 2 = " + (404 & bit) + " → group " + ((404 & bit) != 0 ? "A" : "B"));
        System.out.println("101 & 2 = " + (101 & bit) + " → group " + ((101 & bit) != 0 ? "A" : "B"));
        System.out.println("303 & 2 = " + (303 & bit) + " → group " + ((303 & bit) != 0 ? "A" : "B"));
        // 101 appears twice in same group → cancels
        // 303 appears twice in same group → cancels
    }
}
```

```text
202 & 2 = 2 → group A
404 & 2 = 0 → group B
101 & 2 = 0 → group B
303 & 2 = 2 → group A
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include the two singles being adjacent and the array having only two elements.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class TwoSinglesEdgeCases {

    public static int[] findTwoSingles(int[] nums) {
        int xor = 0; for (int n : nums) xor ^= n;
        int bit = xor & (-xor);
        int a = 0, b = 0;
        for (int n : nums) { if ((n & bit) != 0) a ^= n; else b ^= n; }
        return new int[]{a, b};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Two elements: " + Arrays.toString(findTwoSingles(new int[]{7, 3})));
        System.out.println("Adjacent: " + Arrays.toString(findTwoSingles(new int[]{1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 5})));
    }
}
```

```text
Two elements: [7, 3]
Adjacent: [3, 5]
```

### How is this different from the single number problem?

| Aspect | Single Number | Two Single Numbers |
|---|---|---|
| Unpaired elements | 1 | 2 |
| Passes over data | 1 (XOR all) | 2 (XOR all, then split-XOR) |
| Key insight | XOR cancels pairs | Differing bit splits the two singles |
| Extra space | O(1) | O(1) |
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |

### What about finding three single numbers?

With three unpaired numbers, the XOR of all elements gives the XOR of all three, which does not directly help. More advanced bit manipulation techniques or mathematical properties of XOR are needed. This is significantly harder and less commonly asked.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should explain the distinguishing-bit split clearly: the combined XOR reveals a bit where the two singles differ, and partitioning by that bit separates them. They should know the `x & (-x)` idiom for isolating the rightmost set bit and understand why each paired value falls into the same group and cancels.
